# Speclet Workflow Rules

When working on any speclet-related task:

1. Always read `.speclet/context.md` first — it defines the project stack and conventions
2. If `.speclet/constitution.md` exists and is filled in (no `<!-- speclet:unfilled -->` marker), read it too — it sets project ground rules that must be followed
3. Use `.speclet/tasks/index.md` as the phase map — never guess phase file names
4. Load one phase file at a time from `.speclet/tasks/` — never all at once
5. If `.speclet/architecture.md` exists, read it when implementing features that touch existing modules
6. Follow the matching prompt in `.speclet/prompts/` for each command type:
   - Plan: `.speclet/prompts/plan.md`
   - Map: `.speclet/prompts/map.md`
   - Constitution: `.speclet/prompts/constitution.md`
   - Clarify: `.speclet/prompts/clarify.md`
   - Analyze: `.speclet/prompts/analyze.md`
   - Tasks: `.speclet/prompts/tasks.md`
   - Implement: `.speclet/prompts/implement.md`

## Map (existing projects)
When mapping an existing codebase, explore the project structure, read config and source files,
then write `.speclet/context.md` (stack, conventions) and `.speclet/architecture.md` (module map).

## Implement
When implementing a phase, work through each `- [ ]` task in order, marking each `- [x]` when done.
Do not load other phase files while implementing one phase.
If `.speclet/architecture.md` exists, use it to understand existing module boundaries before making changes.

## Tasks
When generating tasks, write them to the phase file listed in `.speclet/tasks/index.md`.
One phase at a time to minimise token usage.
